menu "Atari 8-bit Bus"

config A8BB_SYSMONITOR
    bool "System monitor (console-key configuration menu)"
    default n
    help
        The configuration menu the PBI driver shows (main/sysMonitor.cpp),
        entered by holding a console key at boot, the 0xE5 key code, or
        every N seconds (config.sysMonitorSec). Its code and data are in
        internal RAM so it can run while core 1 drives the bus: about 6.5 KB
        of static internal RAM plus its menu items on the internal heap.
        Off: none of that is built, and the monitor never starts.

config A8BB_CORE0_HOTSPOTS_EXPLICIT_PLACEMENT
    bool "Explicitly place Core0 IRAM hotspots"
    default n
    help
        Put the Core0 hotspot functions together and align iloop_pbi to the
        next 32 KiB boundary. This guarantees separation from the Core1 loop,
        at the cost of additional IRAM address space and reduced heap space.
        Not with the Sidecar (-DSIDECAR=ON): ldgen orders IRAM code by archive name,
        so libi2slink's hotspots land after the alignment, in the
        Core1 loop's bank, and the post-link check fails the build.

config A8BB_WIFI
    bool "WiFi on main (for its own SMB mounts)"
    default n
    help
        Main's own WiFi station (main/wifi.cpp, wifiPreinit() at boot), for
        the boot profile's smb: drives on a board without a Sidecar. The
        web server, telnet and mDNS are the Sidecar's only (2026-11-21:
        sidecar/main/sidecarNet.cpp; main's telnet is the screen half,
        telnetScreen.cpp, over the link). Off, the linker drops the WiFi
        driver and the PHY blob, whose IRAM (libphy ~5 KB, libpp ~1 KB on
        the S3) no setting moves to flash, and smb: drives stay empty.

config A8BB_REFRESH_GPIO
    int
    default 21
    help
        Where the Atari's refresh_ (active low) reaches main: GPIO21 on
        every board, with or without the Sidecar (its link RX is GPIO18).
        19 (main's USB D-) was the Sidecar builds' while the link RX was
        GPIO21; main's USB-Serial-JTAG pad is then turned off at boot.
        Must be in GPIO_IN_REG (0-31): core 1 tests it in r0. 0: no
        refresh_ (core 1 records refresh cycles, core 0 drops none). No
        prompt (a saved int would keep its old value).

endmenu
